"S1","S2"],"status":"PASS_BOUNDED_SOURCE_AND_CITATION_REVIEW","version":"API0-study-digest-source-review/1","word_count":82,"world_truth_certified":false},"source_input_ref":{"bytes":64013,"path":"data/recovery-studies-v1/digest-inputs/cloze_8c3a7f26c6b8ce2d605fcc8a/4758bc68efb4099753eceb9231b02a54137d2d8991607aff361d338266d8b552.json","sha256":"4758bc68efb4099753eceb9231b02a54137d2d8991607aff361d338266d8b552"},"source_map":[{"canonical_page_id":"page_e9720479cacce1a2569b","citations":[{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_e9720479cacce1a2569b","corpus":"cib","date":"1958-03-29","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003600250001-4","earlier_page":12,"end":658,"page_sha256":"b647cff0ca42735029e996f2a38da012813de184420bddcdce60a1a930e17b0b","start":471,"text":"Kishi, who has given Japan sound leadership, is handicapped by an inability to develop personal popularity, and he is dependent on the achievements of his administration for voter appeal."},"id":"C1"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_e9720479cacce1a2569b","corpus":"cib","date":"1958-03-29","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003600250001-4","earlier_page":12,"end":577,"page_sha256":"b647cff0ca42735029e996f2a38da012813de184420bddcdce60a1a930e17b0b","start":534,"text":"an inability to develop personal popularity"},"id":"C2"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_e9720479cacce1a2569b","corpus":"cib","date":"1958-03-29","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003600250001-4","earlier_page":12,"end":839,"page_sha256":"b647cff0ca42735029e996f2a38da012813de184420bddcdce60a1a930e17b0b","start":471,"text":"Kishi, who has given Japan sound leadership, is handicapped by an inability to develop personal popularity, and he is dependent on the achievements of his administration for voter appeal. He has suggested to Ambassador MacArthur that a favorable US attitude on pending problems, such as a concession to Japan on the Okinawa problem, would strengthen the conservatives."},"id":"C3"}],"corpus":"cib","date":"1958-03-29","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003600250001-4","earlier_page":12,"id":"S1","page_sha256":"b647cff0ca42735029e996f2a38da012813de184420bddcdce60a1a930e17b0b"},{"canonical_page_id":"page_e5702db1bfa1be715132","citations":[{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_e5702db1bfa1be715132","corpus":"cib","date":"1959-01-13","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A004200340001-7","earlier_page":7,"end":1176,"page_sha256":"7eaa148e52576a9988ceae7b800e72ecb620e7892c63fcefa6f7a222413a7587","start":919,"text":"By withholding their participation, prominent dissident leaders are maintaining pressure on Kishi to meet their demands for a complete cabinet reshuffle and for a postponement of the party presidential election until the expiration of Kishi's term in March."},"id":"C1"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_e5702db1bfa1be715132","corpus":"cib","date":"1959-01-13","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A004200340001-7","earlier_page":7,"end":1016,"page_sha256":"7eaa148e52576a9988ceae7b800e72ecb620e7892c63fcefa6f7a222413a7587","start":955,"text":"prominent dissident leaders are maintaining pressure on Kishi"},"id":"C2"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_e5702db1bfa1be715132","corpus":"cib","date":"1959-01-13","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A004200340001-7","earlier_page":7,"end":1327,"page_sha256":"7eaa148e52576a9988ceae7b800e72ecb620e7892c63fcefa6f7a222413a7587","start":838,"text":"The reshuffle points up Kishi's diminishing control of the party and government. By withholding their participation, prominent dissident leaders are maintaining pressure on Kishi to meet their demands for a complete cabinet reshuffle and for a postponement of the party presidential election until the expiration of Kishi's term in March. The postponement could give the dissidents time in which to agree on a candidate to challenge Kishi for the top party post and the prime ministership."},"id":"C3"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_e5702db1bfa1be715132","corpus":"cib","date":"1959-01-13","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A004200340001-7","earlier_page":7,"end":918,"page_sha256":"7eaa148e52576a9988ceae7b800e72ecb620e7892c63fcefa6f7a222413a7587","start":838,"text":"The reshuffle points up Kishi's diminishing control of the party and government."},"id":"C4"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_e5702db1bfa1be715132","corpus":"cib","date":"1959-01-13","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A004200340001-7","earlier_page":7,"end":917,"page_sha256":"7eaa148e52576a9988ceae7b800e72ecb620e7892c63fcefa6f7a222413a7587","start":862,"text":"Kishi's diminishing control of the party and government"},"id":"C5"}],"corpus":"cib","date":"1959-01-13","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A004200340001-7","earlier_page":7,"id":"S2","page_sha256":"7eaa148e52576a9988ceae7b800e72ecb620e7892c63fcefa6f7a222413a7587"},{"canonical_page_id":"page_8653cb966f1591192457","citations":[{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_8653cb966f1591192457","corpus":"cib","date":"1957-10-22","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003300430001-7","earlier_page":11,"end":887,"page_sha256":"72f027162f1c312defa53d4e1f1c349c58d95658582408462c66530b51ef8454","start":656,"text":"His current stumping tour through Japan reveals that despite a record of considerable accomplishments, he has failed to develop wide personal popularity largely because his policies do not have an emotional appeal for the Japanese."},"id":"C1"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_8653cb966f1591192457","corpus":"cib","date":"1957-10-22","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003300430001-7","earlier_page":11,"end":808,"page_sha256":"72f027162f1c312defa53d4e1f1c349c58d95658582408462c66530b51ef8454","start":759,"text":"he has failed to develop wide personal popularity"},"id":"C2"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_8653cb966f1591192457","corpus":"cib","date":"1957-10-22","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003300430001-7","earlier_page":11,"end":1142,"page_sha256":"72f027162f1c312defa53d4e1f1c349c58d95658582408462c66530b51ef8454","start":582,"text":"Kishi is searching for a major triumph before calling national elections. His current stumping tour through Japan reveals that despite a record of considerable accomplishments, he has failed to develop wide personal popularity largely because his policies do not have an emotional appeal for the Japanese. His strong support of a policy of cooperation with the free world and opposition to communism and neutralism runs counter to the popular Japanese desire for greater independence from the United States and closer ties with Asia, including Communist China."},"id":"C3"}],"corpus":"cib","date":"1957-10-22","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003300430001-7","earlier_page":11,"id":"S3","page_sha256":"72f027162f1c312defa53d4e1f1c349c58d95658582408462c66530b51ef8454"}],"text":"The leading suggestion, \u201chis inability to develop personal popularity,\u201d reflects the 29 March 1958 report: Kishi had given Japan sound leadership but depended on administrative achievements for voter appeal [S1]. The alternatives concern dissident leaders\u2019 pressure for a cabinet reshuffle and delayed party election, and Kishi\u2019s diminishing control, as reported on 13 January 1959 [S2]. These reports describe political vulnerabilities, but neither connects them to the Socialists\u2019 encouragement in the concealed passage; the leading suggestion remains contextual rather than a recovered wording.","text_sha256":"4ddf680af9446d847bdd1b5817ce4cf6b6240c5715cb4bca834f2277421e08e4","text_utf8_sha256":"d9fff0c94e0ebd9d79a483beb27c45c98a8ce0bb3478740cb2003041112e8210","used_for_inference":false,"version":"recovery-study-postrun-digest-overlay/1"}